     bboooottppdd [--ii | --ss] [--cc _c_h_d_i_r_-_p_a_t_h] [--dd _l_e_v_e_l] [--hh _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e] [--tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t]
            [_b_o_o_t_p_t_a_b [_d_u_m_p_f_i_l_e]]
     bboooottppggww [--ii | --ss] [--dd _l_e_v_e_l] [--hh _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e] [--tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t] _s_e_r_v_e_r

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The bboooottppdd utility implements an Internet Bootstrap Protocol (BOOTP)
     server as defined in RFC951, RFC1532, and RFC1533.  The bboooottppggww utility
     implements a simple BOOTP gateway which can be used to forward requests
     and responses between clients on one subnet and a BOOTP server (i.e.
     bboooottppdd) on another subnet.  While either bboooottppdd or bboooottppggww will forward
     BOOTREPLY packets, only bboooottppggww will forward BOOTREQUEST packets.

     One host on each network segment is normally configured to run either
     bboooottppdd or bboooottppggww from inetd(8) by including one of the following lines
     in the file _/_e_t_c_/_i_n_e_t_d_._c_o_n_f:

           bootps dgram udp wait root /usr/libexec/bootpd bootpd /etc/bootptab
           bootps dgram udp wait root /usr/libexec/bootpgw bootpgw server

     This mode of operation is referred to as "inetd mode" and causes bboooottppdd
     (or bboooottppggww) to be started only when a boot request arrives.  If it does
     not receive another packet within fifteen minutes of the last one it re‐
     ceived, it will exit to conserve system resources.  The --tt option con‐
     trols this timeout (see OPTIONS).

     It is also possible to run bboooottppdd (or bboooottppggww) in "standalone mode"
     (without inetd(8)) by simply invoking it from a shell like any other reg‐
     ular command.  Standalone mode is particularly useful when bboooottppdd is used
     with a large configuration database, where the start up delay might oth‐
     erwise prevent timely response to client requests.  (Automatic start up
     in standalone mode can be done by invoking bboooottppdd from within
     _/_e_t_c_/_r_c_._l_o_c_a_l, for example.)  Standalone mode is less useful for bboooottppggww
     which has very little start up delay because it does not read a configu‐
     ration file.

     Either program automatically detects whether it was invoked from inetd or
     from a shell and automatically selects the appropriate mode.  The --ss or
     --ii option may be used to force standalone or inetd mode respectively (see
     OPTIONS).

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
     The following options are available:

     --aa      Skip ARP table modifications.

     --tt _t_i_m_e_o_u_t
             Specify the _t_i_m_e_o_u_t value (in minutes) that a bboooottppdd or bboooottppggww
             process will wait for a BOOTP packet before exiting.  If no pack‐
             ets are received for _t_i_m_e_o_u_t minutes, then the program will exit.
             A timeout value of zero means "run forever".  In standalone mode,
             this option is forced to zero.

     --dd _d_e_b_u_g_-_l_e_v_e_l
             Set the _d_e_b_u_g_-_l_e_v_e_l variable that controls the amount of debug‐
             ging messages generated.  For example, --dd4 or --dd 4 will set the
             debugging level to 4.  For compatibility with older versions of
             bboooottppdd, omitting the numeric parameter (i.e., just --dd) will sim‐
             ply increment the debug level by one.

     --cc _c_h_d_i_r_-_p_a_t_h
             Set the current directory used by bboooottppdd while checking the exis‐
             tence and size of client boot files.  This is useful when client
             boot files are specified as relative pathnames, and bboooottppdd needs
             to use the same current directory as the TFTP server (typically
             _/_t_f_t_p_b_o_o_t).  This option is not recognized by bboooottppggww.

     --hh _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e
             Specify the hostname corresponding to the IP address to listen
             on.  By default, bboooottppdd listens on the IP address corresponding
             to the machine's hostname, as returned by gethostname(3).

     --ii      Force inetd mode.  This option is obsolete, but remains for com‐
             patibility with older versions of bboooottppdd.

     --ss      Force standalone mode.  This option is obsolete, but remains for
             compatibility with older versions of bboooottppdd.

     _b_o_o_t_p_t_a_b
             Specify the name of the configuration file from which bboooottppdd
             loads its database of known clients and client options (bboooottppdd
             only).

     _d_u_m_p_f_i_l_e
             Specify the name of the file that bboooottppdd will dump its internal
             database into when it receives a SIGUSR1 signal (bboooottppdd only).
             This option is only recognized if bboooottppdd was compiled with the
             -DDEBUG flag.

     _s_e_r_v_e_r  Specify the name of a BOOTP server to which bboooottppggww will forward
             all BOOTREQUEST packets it receives (bboooottppggww only).

OOPPEERRAATTIIOONN
     Both bboooottppdd and bboooottppggww operate similarly in that both listen for any
     packets sent to the _b_o_o_t_p_s port, and both simply forward any BOOTREPLY
     packets.  They differ in their handling of BOOTREQUEST packets.

     When bboooottppggww is started, it determines the address of a BOOTP server
     whose name is provided as a command line parameter.  When bboooottppggww re‐
     ceives a BOOTREQUEST packet, it sets the "gateway address" and "hop
     count" fields in the packet and forwards the packet to the BOOTP server
     at the address determined earlier.  Requests are forwarded only if they
     indicate that the client has been waiting for at least three seconds.

     When bboooottppdd is started it reads a configuration file, (normally
     _/_e_t_c_/_b_o_o_t_p_t_a_b) that initializes the internal database of known clients
     and client options.  This internal database is reloaded from the configu‐
     ration file when bboooottppdd receives a hangup signal (SIGHUP) or when it dis‐
     covers that the configuration file has changed.

     When bboooottppdd receives a BOOTREQUEST packet, it looks for a database entry
     matching the client request.  If the client is known, bboooottppdd composes a
     BOOTREPLY packet using the database entry found above, and sends the re‐
     ply to the client (possibly using a gateway).  If the client is unknown,
     the request is discarded (with a notice if debug > 0).

     If bboooottppdd is compiled with the -DDEBUG option, receipt of a SIGUSR1 sig‐
     nal causes it to dump its internal database to the file _/_t_m_p_/_b_o_o_t_p_d_._d_u_m_p
     or the dumpfile specified as a command line parameter.

     During initialization, both programs determine the UDP port numbers to be
     used by calling getservbyname(3) (which normally uses _/_e_t_c_/_s_e_r_v_i_c_e_s).
     Two service names (and port numbers) are used:

           bootps BOOTP Server listening port
           bootpc BOOTP Client destination port

     If the port numbers cannot be determined using getservbyname(3) then the
     values default to bootps=67 and bootpc=68.

FFIILLEESS
     /etc/bootptab     Database file read by bboooottppdd.
     /tmp/bootpd.dump  Debugging dump file created by bboooottppdd.
     /etc/services     Internet service numbers.
     /tftpboot         Current directory typically used by the TFTP server and
                       bboooottppdd.

